// A user who only has folder-level access to a sibling folder (f2,
// distinct from d's actual parent f1) should never be able to read a
// document filed under f1.
//
// Before `docs/DECISIONS.md`'s entry adding `NeverRelationConstraint`,
// this invariant's own real verdict was VIOLATED: nothing stopped the
// search from adding one more, wholly unconstrained direct grant of
// `doc.viewer` (bare principal) to `u` — the exact "recurring finding"
// `docs/FINDINGS.md` disclosed. Three further, independent escapes
// survived even after excluding that one specific grant (D-131's own
// narrow primitive): a completely separate top-level union term,
// `doc.owner`, unrelated to `viewer`; and, via the invariant's own given
// `parent(d) = f1`, `folder.viewer_direct` and `folder.owner` reachable
// up an unconstrained ancestor chain. `never doc#viewer(u)`, `never
// doc#owner(u)`, `never folder#owner(u)`, and `never
// folder#viewer_direct(u)` close all four — `folder.viewer_direct`'s own
// line closes the recursive `parent->viewer` ancestor chain at every
// depth, since every step still has to resolve `folder.viewer_direct`'s
// own direct edge somewhere. `u`'s real, legitimate grant on `f2` (this
// invariant's own given premise) is exempted, not blocked, and the
// negative control confirms the exemption is precise: pinning the given
// to `f1` (the object the invariant means to probe) instead of `f2`
// still correctly reports VIOLATED. See `docs/FINDINGS.md` for the full
// account.
invariant sibling_folder_viewer_cannot_read_document {
  u: user
  f1: folder
  f2: folder
  d: doc

  parent(d) = f1
  viewer_direct(f2) = u
  distinct(f1, f2)

  never doc#viewer(u)
  never doc#owner(u)
  never folder#owner(u)
  never folder#viewer_direct(u)

  goal: can_read(u, d)
}
